"EmbeddingGemma 2 model card  |  Google AI for Developers (https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/embeddinggemma/model_card_2)\nciteturn41358view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/embeddinggemma/model_card_2\",\"lineno\":220}); Total lines: 368\nL149: Output Dimension  | Compression Ratio  | MTEB (multilingual, v2) Mean(Task)  | MTEB (eng, v2) Mean(Task)  | MTEB (code, v1) Mean(Task)  | MIEB (lite) Mean(TaskType)  | MMEB (v2) Overall  | MSEB (Retrieval) Mean(Task)  | MAEB Mean(Task)\nL150: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL151: 768d (Full)  | 1:1  | 61.36  | 68.46  | 78.68  | 64.64  | 59.01  | 69.54  | 49.39\nL152: 512d  | 1:1.5  | 61.17  | 68.41  | 77.24  | 64.32  | 58.38  | 69.18  | 49.21\nL153: 256d  | 1:3  | 60.41  | 67.78  | 76.18  | 63.13  | 56.24  | 66.76  | 48.91\nL154: 128d  | 1:6  | 57.89  | 65.68  | 71.41  | 59.06  | 45.65  | 56.71  | 46.92\nL155: ## Quick Start\nL156: \nL157: Install the `sentence-transformers` library:\nL158: \nL159:     `pip install -U sentence-transformers transformers\nL160:     `\nL161: \nL162: Generate text embeddings: ```python from sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nL163: \nL164: model = SentenceTransformer(\"google/embeddinggemma-2\")\nL165: \nL166: query = \"What causes the northern lights?\" document = \"The northern lights are caused by charged particles from the sun.\"\nL167: query_emb = model.encode(query, prompt_name=\"SearchQuery\") doc_emb = model.encode(document, prompt_name=\"Document\") print(model.similarity(query_emb, doc_emb)) ```\nL168: ## Best Practices\nL169: \nL170: For optimal embedding quality and runtime efficiency, follow these configurations and best practices:\nL171: ### 1. Task Instruction Prefixes\nL172: \nL173: EmbeddingGemma 2 is trained with short task instruction prefixes prepended to text inputs. Using the right prefix improves quality; omitting it still works but reduces precision. Prefixes apply to text only. Pass images, video, and audio without any prefix.\nL174: \nL175: Documents with a real title should be formatted as `title: {title} | text: {content}`. Use `title: none` when no title is available.\nL176: \nL177: Prefix Notation & Usage\nL178: We offer two types of task prefixes, depending on how embeddings are used in the task. There are two types of tasks:\nL179:   * Asymmetric Tasks (e.g. retrieval): Use a query prefix for queries and a document prefix for corpus items.\nL180:   * Symmetric Tasks (e.g. classification, similarity): Apply the same task prefix to all inputs being compared.\nL181: Use Case  | Task Type  | Prompt Name  | Query Task Instruction  | Document Task Instruction (use `none` if no title)\nL182: --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL183: Web / document search  | Asymmetric  | SearchQuery  | `task: search result | query: {query}`  | `title: {title} | text: {content}`\nL184: Question answering  | Asymmetric  | QuestionAnswering  | `task: question answering | query: {question}`  | `title: {title} | text: {passage}`\nL185: Fact checking  | Asymmetric  | FactChecking  | `task: fact checking | query: {claim}`  | `title: {title} | text: {evidence}`\nL186: Code search  | Asymmetric  | CodeRetrieval  | `task: code retrieval | query: {query}`  | `title: {title or filename} | text: {code}`\nL187: Text classification  | Symmetric  | Classification  | `task: classification | query: {content}`  | N/A\nL188: Clustering  | Symmetric  | Clustering  | `task: clustering | query: {content}`  | N/A\nL189: Measuring similarity  | Symmetric  | SentenceSimilarity  | `task: sentence similarity | query: {content}`  | N/A\nL190: Please note: `prompt_name=\"Document\"` applies `title: none`; titled documents must still be formatted manually, like `model.encode(f\"title: {title} | text: {document}\")`\nL191: ### 2. Selective Encoder Loading\nL192: \nL193: The vision and audio encoders are independent components. To reduce memory consumption when deploying text-only or single-modality pipelines, disable unused modality encoders via SentenceTransformer's `config_kwargs`:\nL194: \nL195: Please note: configuring EmbeddingGemma 2 to load with omission of some encoders differs among model libraries; please refer to the appropriate documentation for more information on this.\nL196: Active Modalities  | `config_kwargs`  | Effective Size\nL197: --- | --- | ---\nL198: Text only  | `{\"vision_config\": None, \"audio_config\": None}`  | 270M\nL199: Text and image  | `{\"audio_config\": None}`  | 440M\nL200: Text and audio  | `{\"vision_config\": None}`  | 570M\nL201: Full multimodal  | `{}`  | 740M\nL202: ### 3. Matryoshka Dimension Truncation\nL203: \nL204: EmbeddingGemma 2 is trained with Matryoshka Representation Learning, so the 768-dimensional output vector can be shortened by keeping only its leading dimensions. The supported dimensions are 768, 512, 256, and 128. Shorter vectors reduce storage and speed up similarity search at some cost to quality.\nL205: \nL206: At runtime, please adhere to the following guidelines:\nL207:   * Re-normalize after truncating: slicing a unit-length vector does not preserve unit length. The shortened vector must be L2-normalized before it is used for cosine similarity. Skipping this step degrades ranking quality silently—it produces plausible-looking scores rather than an error.\nL208:   * Queries and documents must share a dimension. A 768-dimensional query cannot be scored against a 128-dimensional corpus.\nL209:   * To avoid truncating and normalizing yourself, you should pass in the `truncate_dim` and `normalize_embeddings` fields when calling `model.encode()`:\nL210: \nL211:     `query_emb = model.encode(\nL212:        query,\nL213:        truncate_dim=128, # or 512, 256\nL214:        normalize_embeddings=True,\nL215:     )\nL216:     `\nL217: Model quality at each dimension is reported in the truncation table in the Benchmark Results section above. Quality is close to lossless down to 256 dimensions. 128 dimensions degrades multimodal quality substantially and should be validated against your own workload before adoption.\nL218: ### 4. Numerical Precision\nL219: \nL220: Run inference in `bfloat16` or `float32`. Do not use `float16`.\nL221: \nL222: EmbeddingGemma 2's activation range exceeds the dynamic range of `float16`. In `float16` the model returns NaN or silently degraded embeddings rather than raising an error, so the failure is easy to miss.\nL223: `bfloat16` is safe, and carries the same 8-bit exponent as `float32`. This is the recommended default on hardware with native support, and halves memory use relative to `float32`. Use `float32` elsewhere, including on most CPUs.\nL224: In SentenceTransformers, you can set this via `model_kwargs`, and determine it programmatically via `is_bf16_supported`:\nL225: \nL226:     `dtype = torch.bfloat16 if torch.cuda.is_bf16_supported() else torch.float32\nL227: \nL228:     model = SentenceTransformer(\"google/embeddinggemma-2\", model_kwargs={\"torch_dtype\": dtype})\nL229:     `\nL230: ### 5. Multimodal Input\nL231: #### Interleaving\nL232: \nL233: A single input may mix text with images, video, and audio, using a single shared 8,192-token context.\nL234: \nL235: The position of each media item within the sequence is marked in the text using placeholder tokens from the model's vocabulary:\nL236: \nL237:   * `<|image|>` marks the position of image input\nL238:   * `<|video|>` marks the position of video input\nL239:   * `<|audio|>` marks the position of audio input\nL240: For example, a product listing indexed for search might be encoded as:\nL241: \nL242:     `emb_interleaved = model.encode({\nL243:         \"text\": \"Waterproof running shoes. <|image|> Featuring a breathable mesh upper. <|image|> Grip test on wet rock: <|video|>\",\nL244:         \"image\": [\"shoe.jpg\", \"mesh.jpg\"],\nL245:         \"video\": \"demo.mp4\",\nL246:     })\nL247:     `\nL248: Each placeholder in text is filled from the corresponding key, in order. The call returns a single embedding that represents the text, images and video together. This embedding can be compared directly against any other EmbeddingGemma 2 embedding; for example, a text-only query such as \"waterproof shoes for trail running\".\nL249: #### Context Limits\nL250: \nL251: All modalities share a single 8,192-token context window. Each modality consumes that budget at a fixed rate:\nL252: \nL253: Modality  | Token Cost  | Max Input\nL254: --- | --- | ---\nL255: Text  | 1 token per subword  | 8,192 tokens\nL256: Image  | 280 tokens per image (default)  | ~29 images\nL257: Video  | 140 tokens per frame (default)  | ~58 frames\nL258: Audio  | 25 tokens per second  | ~327 seconds\nL259: Note max input for images and video can be as high as ~114 images or frames when using a lower vision token budget (described below).\nL260: \nL261: The maximums above assume a single modality with no accompanying text. Interleaved inputs draw from the same budget, so mixing modalities reduces the amount of each that fits. Note also max input for images and video can be as high as ~114 images or frames when using a lower vision token budget (described below).\nL262: #### Vision Token Budget\nL263: \nL264: Model users can forgo the default sequence length for input images / video frames to represent images with “soft token” amounts ranging from 70 to 1120. Increasing the vision budget for input images trades latency and token count for quality.\nL265: In general, higher input sequence lengths capture more information. Scaling up input sequence length means more expressive input representations; so, increasing input sequence length will improve fine-grained visual understanding, uplifting embedding quality and performance in downstream use cases.\nL266: #### Input Sampling Defaults\nL267: \nL268: Audio and video inputs have the following default sampling rates:\nL269: \nL270:   * By default, video is processed as sampled frames through the vision encoder at 1 frame per second; FPS sampling rate is configurable.\nL271:   * Audio should be supplied as mono at 16 kHz.\nL272: ## Model Data\nL273: \nL274: Our pre-training dataset is a large-scale, diverse collection of data encompassing a wide range of domains and modalities, which includes web documents, code, images, video, and audio, with a cutoff date of January 2025. These include:\nL275:   * Web Documents: A diverse collection of web text ensures the model is exposed to a broad range of linguistic styles, topics, and vocabulary. The training dataset includes content in over 140 languages.\nL276:   * Code: Exposing the model to code helps it to learn the syntax and patterns of programming languages, which improves its ability to understand code-related semantics.\nL277:   * Images: A wide range of images enables the model to perform image analysis and visual data extraction tasks.\nL278:   * Video: Video sequences and clips covering diverse visual scenes, human actions, and temporal dynamics.\nL279:   * Audio: Speech recordings across multiple languages, environmental sounds, and acoustic events.\nL280:   * Cross-modality Samples: Paired text, image, video, and audio data to align representations across different modalities.\nL281: ### Data Processing\nL282: \nL283: Several data cleaning and filtering methods were applied to the training data:\nL284:   * CSAM Filtering: Rigorous CSAM (Child Sexual Abuse Material) filtering was applied at multiple stages in the data preparation process to ensure the exclusion of harmful and illegal content.\nL285:   * Sensitive Data Filtering: As part of making Gemma pre-trained models safe and reliable, automated techniques were used to filter out certain personal information and other sensitive data from training sets.\nL286:   * Additional Methods: Filtering based on content quality and safety in line with cite15†our policies†ai.google .\nL287: ### Ethics & Safety\nL288: \nL289: EmbeddingGemma 2 is a pre-trained embedding model. Unlike generative models, it does not undergo post-training alignment, safety tuning, or output-level moderation. Safety mitigations during development were focused on pre-training data filtering to reduce exposure to harmful content and severe biases in the learned embedding space, in alignment with cite16†Google's AI Principles†ai.google .\nL290: Since embedding models produce representations rather than user-facing text, safety risks manifest downstream in how those representations are used.\nL291: \nL292: Developers and deployers are responsible for evaluating and implementing application-level safeguards, like retrieval filtering and fairness testing, appropriate to their specific production use case.\nL293: \nL294: Deployments must adhere to the cite17†Gemma Prohibited Use Policy .\nL295: ## Usage and Limitations\nL296: \nL297: EmbeddingGemma 2 has certain limitations that users should be aware of.\nL298: ### Intended Usage\nL299: \nL300: EmbeddingGemma 2 generates embeddings from input content, which can be used for a number of downstream applications.\nL301: \nL302: The following list of potential uses is not comprehensive. The purpose of this list is to provide contextual information about the possible use-cases that the model creators considered as part of model training and development.\nL303:   * Retrieval: Embeddings for semantic search across text, code, images, video, and audio, such as document search, RAG over enterprise knowledge bases, code search from natural language queries, or spoken-query search over audio archives.\nL304:   * Classification: Embeddings to classify inputs according to preset labels, like sentiment analysis, content moderation, audio event detection, or image categorization.\nL305:   * Clustering: Embeddings to group inputs based on semantic similarity, like organizing document collections, clustering customer feedback by theme, or discovering near-duplicate content across modalities.\nL306:   * Semantic Similarity: Embeddings to measure pairwise similarity between inputs, such as recommendation systems, duplicate detection, paraphrase identification, or cross-lingual content alignment.\nL307:   * Fact Verification: Embeddings for retrieving evidence documents given a claim, like automated fact-checking systems or source attribution pipelines.\nL308: ### Limitations\nL309:   * Training Data: The quality and diversity of the training data significantly influence the model's capabilities. Biases or gaps in the training data can lead to limitations in the model's responses.\nL310:     * The scope of the training dataset determines the subject areas the model can handle effectively.\nL311:     * For example, while EmbeddingGemma 2 supports 100+ languages, the model may not exhibit equal performance across languages.\nL312:   * Context and Task Complexity: Models perform well on tasks that can be framed with clear prompts and instructions.\nL313:     * Open-ended or highly complex tasks might be challenging.\nL314:     * A model's performance can be influenced by the amount of context provided (longer context generally leads to better outputs, up to a certain point).\nL315:   * Language Ambiguity and Nuance: Natural language is inherently complex. Models might struggle to grasp subtle nuances, sarcasm, or figurative language.\nL316:   * Task Instruction Usage: For text tasks, omitting the recommended task prefix may lead to sub-optimal embedding quality.\nL317: ### Ethical Considerations and Risks\nL318: \nL319: In creating an open embedding model, we have carefully considered the following:\nL320:   * Bias and Fairness\nL321:     * Models trained on large-scale, real-world text and image data can reflect socio-cultural biases embedded in the training material.\nL322:     * Training data used for EmbeddingGemma 2 underwent safety filtering to mitigate the risk of these biases.\nL323:   * Misinformation and Misuse\nL324:     * Embedding representations can be misused to retrieve, classify, or otherwise organize embedded content in false, misleading or harmful ways.\nL325:     * Guidelines are provided for responsible use with the model, see the cite18†Responsible Generative AI Toolkit .\nL326:   * Transparency and Accountability\nL327:     * This model card summarizes details on the model's architecture, capabilities, limitations, and evaluation processes.\nL328:     * A responsibly developed open model offers the opportunity to share innovation by making Vision-Language Model (VLM) technology accessible to developers and researchers across the AI ecosystem.\nL329: ### Risks Identified and Mitigations\nL330:   * Misuse for malicious purposes: Technical limitations and developer and end-user education can help mitigate against malicious applications of embedding models. Educational resources and reporting mechanisms for users to flag misuse are provided.\nL331:   * Privacy violations: Models were trained on data filtered for removal of certain personal information and other sensitive data. Developers are encouraged to adhere to privacy regulations with privacy-preserving techniques.\nL332:   * Perpetuation of biases: It's encouraged to perform continuous monitoring (using evaluation metrics, human review) and the exploration of de-biasing techniques during model training, fine-tuning, and other use cases.\nL333: ### Benefits\nL334: \nL335: EmbeddingGemma 2 is among the strongest multimodal embedding models under 1B parameters. We’re excited to see how developers will use and adapt this model for on-device or edge AI applications.\nL336: \nL337: The model is designed from the ground up for responsible AI development, like other Gemma-family models.\nL338: \nL339: [Button: Send feedback ]\nL340: Except as otherwise noted, the content of this page is licensed under the cite19†Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 License†creativecommons.org , and code samples are licensed under the cite20†Apache 2.0 License†www.apache.org . For details, see the cite21†Google Developers Site Policies†developers.google.com . Java is a registered trademark of Oracle and/or its affiliates.\nL341: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBring multimodal semantic search to the edge with EmbeddingGemma 2 - Google Developers Blog (https://developers.googleblog.com/google-ai-edge-with-embeddinggemma-2/)\nciteturn41358view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developers.googleblog.com/google-ai-edge-with-embeddinggemma-2/\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 147\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Community/Events†developers.google.com L3: \nL4: cite2†Learn†developers.google.com L5: \nL6: cite3†Blog L7: \nL8: cite4†YouTube†www.youtube.com L9: \nL10: [Input: Search all articles...]\nL11: \nL12: [Button: Search ]\nL13: # Bring multimodal semantic search to the edge with EmbeddingGemma 2\nL14: \nL15: OCT. 6, 2026\nL16: \nL17: cite5†Google AI Edge Team L18: \nL19: cite6†Android ML Team L20: Today, Google DeepMind launched EmbeddingGemma 2, a best-in-class for its size, open-weight, multimodal embedding model that natively maps text, images, video frames, and audio into a single, unified vector space. For developers building local search and media retrieval experiences, it reduces the latency and memory overhead of chaining separate image captioning, speech-to-text, and text-embedding models.\nL21: Designed specifically for local, privacy-first applications, EmbeddingGemma 2 covers text, vision, and audio modalities in a compact 740M parameter footprint, offering modular encoders that can run on as little as ~191MB active RAM for text-only weights and ~567MB for the full multimodal model on a Google Pixel 11 Pro. Most notably, EmbeddingGemma 2 can act as an ultra-low-latency on-device decision engine.\nL22: Without requiring any training data or fine-tuning, it matches user inputs directly against classification labels and descriptions, delivering instant, zero-shot intent routing in a matter of milliseconds. For an overview of the model architecture and evals including multimodal embedding capabilities, check out the cite7†Google DeepMind blog†blog.google .\nL23: In this post, we’ll share how you can experience the power of EmbeddingGemma 2 right away through cite8†Google AI Edge’s†developers.google.com interactive showcases, cite9†Google AI Edge Gallery†developers.google.com on mobile and cite10†Google AI Edge Foresight†developers.google.com on Mac, and how you can leverage the model to build your own private, on-device semantic search, visual keyframe retrieval, and condition-trigger workflows.\nL24: In the coming weeks, we will also make this model available as a service on Android, accessible through cite11†ML Kit†developers.google.com , including using NPU acceleration to further optimize performance across a broad range of devices.\nL25: ## Instant Media Search and Video Moments Finder in Google AI Edge Gallery\nL26: \nL27: cite9†Google AI Edge Gallery†developers.google.com is a cross-platform showcase application designed to allow users and developers to experience and evaluate on-device AI models and their capabilities in practical settings. Today, we are adding two interactive showcases powered by EmbeddingGemma 2 to the Google AI Edge Gallery app: Instant Media Search and Video Moments Finder.\nL28: Instant Media Search allows users to find specific images or videos in their local media using natural language or example images. By converting both input query and the media into on-device embedding vectors - with the latter stored in a local SQLite database - the system retrieves relevant content by returning those with the largest cosine similarity. This enables an instant, interactive search experience as the user types.\nL29: \nL30: Sorry, your browser doesn't support playback for this video\nL31: Search-as-you-type and image-to-image retrieval\nL32:   * On-device image embedding calculation and fast multimodal retrieval: Whether you are starting with the sample image set provided by the app or testing instant search on your own media, you can experience how quickly EmbeddingGemma 2 executes embedding computations locally on your phone, no internet connection required.\nL33:   * Multimodal retrieval and similarity matching: Because query token embedding and cosine similarity ranking execute quickly on device using MediaPipe cite12†UniversalEmbedder†developers.google.com and cite13†SemanticRetriever†developers.google.com , search results update live on every keystroke.\nL34: As soon as you type \"Katze\" (\"Cat\" in German), the grid immediately displays all feline photos in your collection; continuing to type \"Katze schläft auf Tastatur\" (\"Cat sleeping on keyboard\" in German) dynamically re-ranks and brings the specific photo you have in mind to the top in real time. Besides text queries in multiple languages, users can also select any image from their phone or use their live camera stream to retrieve visually and semantically related photos.\nL35: Video Moments Finder demonstrates another example of how EmbeddingGemma 2's cross-modal representations can be applied, enabling users to locate specific visual moments across local video recordings without transcribing audio or generating intermediate text captions.\nL36: \nL37: Sorry, your browser doesn't support playback for this video\nL38: \nL39: Search local video files for specific moments\nL40:   * On-device video keyframe indexing and natural language search: After users select a local video (such as a family video or sports reel), the on-device engine indexes vision with audio chunks and generates embeddings for them.\nL41: When users enter descriptive queries, such as \"kids laughing\", \"dog catching a frisbee\", or \"person blowing out birthday candles,\" the engine computes the prompt embedding vector, compares it against the video frame embedding, and instantly highlights the exact timestamps where matching moments occur.\nL42: Download the latest version of cite9†Google AI Edge Gallery app†developers.google.com from cite14†Google Play Store†play.google.com or cite15†Apple App Store†apps.apple.com to try these new demos. Check-out the cite16†Github repo†github.com to learn how it’s implemented and start building your own experiences using EmbeddingGemma 2.\nL43: ## Productivity boost and context retrieval in Google AI Edge Foresight on Mac\nL44: We are excited to announce the launch of cite10†Google AI Edge Foresight†developers.google.com for Mac, an experimental app that brings a context-aware meeting companion directly to your device. Powered by fully local AI processing, Foresight seamlessly assists with note-taking, indexing, and recalling your conversation transcripts and private files. By integrating directly with your system audio and microphone, it works out-of-the-box with any meeting platform, even completely offline.\nL45: Sorry, your browser doesn't support playback for this video\nL46: \nL47: Automatic enhancement of shorthand notes\nL48: \nL49: Sorry, your browser doesn't support playback for this video\nL50: \nL51: Questions detected and answered live by AI\nL52: \nL53: Powered by EmbeddingGemma 2 and Gemma 4 models, Foresight delivers:\nL54:   * Enhanced Note-taking: Enriches manual shorthand notes with details retrieved in real-time from your current meeting conversation.\nL55:   * Cross-Modal Retrieval: Easily find different types of media (images, documents, transcripts, and notes) using natural language.\nL56:   * On-device Benefits: Your sensitive information remains secure, along with uninterrupted productivity regardless of your network connection, and with no cloud subscription costs.\nL57: By mapping text, audio, and visual data into a single vector space, Foresight runs local, secure searches across your personal knowledge library. The details you need are always at your fingertips, whether you are answering live questions mid-meeting or drafting thorough notes, while your sensitive data never leaves your device.\nL58: \nL59: cite10†Download†developers.google.com the Google AI Edge Foresight Mac app today, and bring an intelligent, local companion to all your meetings.\nL60: ## Build end-to-end search with ML Kit and MediaPipe Tasks\nL61: \nL62: Building For Android with ML Kit\nL63: For developers building for the Android ecosystem, EmbeddingGemma 2 is a lightweight, task-specific model perfectly suited for on-device execution. We are bringing these unified search capabilities to ML Kit in the coming weeks, providing a standardized, platform-integrated path for production Android apps in the future. By integrating with ML Kit developers can get production-grade quality for EmbeddingGemma out of the box without having to manage the model or worrying about APK bloat.\nL64: ML Kit will feature blazing fast performance through NPU acceleration (when available) and automatic model updates.\nL65: Building For X-Platform with MediaPipe Tasks\nL66: \nL67: Integrating local search features, similar to those in Google AI Edge Gallery and Foresight above, usually requires developers to handle low-level media pre-processing for the data to be fed into the embedding model. To simplify this, MediaPipe Tasks is adding support for EmbeddingGemma 2 to the existing Embedder and Semantic Retriever; the new interface to our current RAG SDK.\nL68: Whether you are targeting iOS, macOS, Windows, Linux or Web, MediaPipe Tasks provide a unified developer experience to write once and deploy across all platforms. Instead of developers manually managing pre-processing and data transformations, the Embedder and Semantic Retriever Tasks provide a turnkey solution that completely abstracts these steps.\nL69:   * cite12†MediaPipe Universal Embedder Task†developers.google.com : Automatically handles image resizing, tensor normalization, and multimodal tokenization. Pass raw images or text strings directly to receive 768-dimensional (or truncated 128d–512d) normalized vectors with zero boilerplate.\nL70:   * cite13†MediaPipe Semantic Retriever Task†developers.google.com : An edge-optimized embedded vector search engine indexes embeddings on-device and runs fast Approximate Nearest Neighbour (ANN) searches, returning ranked matches in single-digit milliseconds.\nL71: EmbeddingGemma 2 can also be used as a multimodal, real-time decision engine with the cite17†MediaPipe Decision Task†developers.google.com . This allows developers to effortlessly classify image, text or audio inputs without the need for fine-tuning and make instant decisions on-device, enabling use cases like routing or action prediction.\nL72: The example below shows the MediaPipe Decision task running with less than 100ms response time to evaluate 500 options each turn in the chess game, showcasing the efficiency of EmbeddingGemma 2 as the decision engine fully on-device.\nL73: Sorry, your browser doesn't support playback for this video\nL74: \nL75: EmbeddingGemma 2 powers low-latency MediaPipe Decision task on-device\nL76: ## Building cross-platform apps using EmbeddingGemma 2 with LiteRT\nL77: While MediaPipe Tasks offers turnkey simplicity, cross-platform applications requiring fine-grained control over processing and acceleration can be built directly on cite18†LiteRT†developers.google.com , the proven engine powering ML Kit, MediaPipe Tasks, Google AI Edge Gallery, and Foresight.\nL78: Powered by cite19†unified acceleration across CPUs, GPUs, and NPUs, deployment is greatly simplified with exceptional portability: a single `.litertlm` file runs out of the box on both CPU and GPU across all supported edge platforms.\nL79: On Android, ML Kit APIs (coming soon) leverage LiteRT to provide optimized implementation for the platform, giving you both performance and simplicity without having to worry about model lifecycle management.\nL80: To provide the best inference speed for EmbeddingGemma 2, we implemented targeted optimizations for compute-intensive workloads like vision encoding. This unlocks highly responsive, real-time AI experiences, as “search-as-you-type” in the Google AI Edge Gallery app, with visual embeddings taking as little as 37.3 ms (26.9 images per sec) on a MacBook M5 Pro GPU. See table for additional measurements.\nL81: \nL82: cite20†Image: AI Edge EGv2 Blog (3)†storage.googleapis.com L83: Table: Per image EmbeddingGemma 2 vision latency across CPU, GPU and NPU for a representative set of devices. Latency column specifies the vision embedding latency, evaluated with a maximum budget of 70 vision tokens per image.\nL84: ## Architectural optimizations under the hood\nL85: \nL86: Achieving high-performance execution for EmbeddingGemma 2 on memory-constrained edge devices is enabled by the following optimizations:\nL87:   * Modularity across modalities: Load only the encoders you need (e.g., Text + Vision for photo and video search, or dynamically activate the audio encoder when voice memos are added) to maximize edge efficiency and manage system memory pressure.\nL88:   * Quantization-Aware Training (QAT): Compresses model weights to INT4 and INT8, bringing multimodal vector search within reach of mid-range consumer hardware.\nL89:   * Adaptive input and output scaling: Given the text and image input size, we dynamically load the optimal computational graph at runtime, consistently supported across all CPU, GPU, and NPU backends. This design accommodates variable text length without wasted compute while empowering developers to control vision token budgets for the ideal balance between latency and precision.\nL90: For output, built-in cite21†Matryoshka Representation Learning†arxiv.org (MRL) slicing allows on-the-fly dimension truncation, cutting local storage and index footprints by up to 8x.\nL91: LiteRT delivers robust, cross-platform performance across a broad spectrum of edge devices and web (WASM). For a complete list of supported hardware platforms and the latest performance benchmarks, see our Hugging Face cite22†model card†huggingface.co . You can try the cite23†LiteRT benchmark†github.com tool with more physical devices on Google Cloud or check out our cite24†zero-setup web demo†google-ai-edge.github.io for an instant, browser-based playground.\nL92: ## Get started today\nL93: \nL94: EmbeddingGemma 2 and Google AI Edge provide the building blocks to bring fast, private, and offline multimodal retrieval directly into your applications. Whether you’re experimenting with instant image search, visual keyframe retrieval, or ambient condition triggers, all the tools you need are available today.\nL95:   * Experience the EmbeddingGemma 2 demos: Try Instant Media Search and Video Moments Finder on your own device with the cite9†Google AI Edge Gallery App†developers.google.com on cite14†Android†play.google.com and cite15†iOS†apps.apple.com , and see personal knowledge base retrieval in action with cite10†AI Edge Foresight†developers.google.com .\nL96:   * Benchmark EmbeddingGemma 2: Benchmark and develop features with EmbeddingGemma 2 across over 200 different real physical devices in Google Cloud’s cite25†Developer Device Platform†cloud.google.com . Get started with our interactive cite23†Google Colab and guide†github.com on Github.\nL97:   * Deploy EmbeddingGemma 2: Download ready-to-run, pre-quantized `.litertlm` bundles directly from the cite26†Hugging Face LiteRT Community†huggingface.co .\nL98:   * Start building: Explore cite27†MediaPipe Tasks†developers.google.com for turnkey integrations or follow the cite28†LiteRT-LM developer guide†developers.google.com for more custom implementations. Explore the reference implementations in the cite16†Google AI Edge Gallery app†github.com .\nL99: ### Acknowledgements\nL100: \nL101: ^{We'd like to extend a